\newlabel{fig:binary_numerics}{{1}{33}{Exact computations on small instances. \textbf {(a)} $\Gap (K_\lambda )$ along the tempering path~\eqref {eq:binary_path} for three signal strengths; the shaded band is the union of the intervals of Proposition~\ref {prop:binary_badset} on which the margin condition~\eqref {eq:binary_margin} fails, for the strongest signal; the dotted line is the bound $1/(64ds_0)$ of Theorem~\ref {th:binary_gap_margin}. \textbf {(b)} $\Gap (K_1)$ and $\min _{\lambda \in [0,1]}\Gap (K_\lambda )$ as the signal strength sweeps through the range in which the posterior mass $\pi (x^{\star }\mid y)$ (dotted, right axis) moves from $0$ to $1$. \textbf {(c)} $\Gap (K_1)$ as a function of the truncation level $s_0$ for the example of \citet [Appendix~B]{YangWainwrightJordan2016}: $d=n=12$ and $y=w$ pure noise. \textbf {(d)} the variance function $V$ of~\eqref {eq:binary_V} and the schedule~\eqref {eq:binary_schedule} it produces (dots)}{figure.caption.2}{}}
